  description:
  - 'While most insects glow by using forms of bioluminescience, these creatures
    achieve their glow via a flammable waxy substance that coats their abdomen. They
    use their high body heat to burn down suitable objects into ash, from which they
    ingest using their probiscis. '
  - Their bodies cannot ignite on their own, and require a nearby heat source (often
    being their home hive) to reignite if they were to be extinguished.
  leftText: 
  rightText: While most insects glow by using forms of bioluminescience, these creatures
    achieve their light via a flammable waxy substance that coats their abdomen.
    They use their high body heat to burn down suitable objects into ash (often times
    plant matter), from which they ingest using their probiscis. Their bodies cannot
    ignite on their own, and require a nearby heat source to reignite if extinguished.
